Decisions: SDD

Purpose:
  Define how human decisions are requested, recorded, approved, rejected,
  superseded and applied inside the SDD system. This is the layer that
  manages the POINTS where AI needs a human decision — it is not a place
  where AI records its own decisions (see Note below for the full
  distinction; this is the single most important thing to understand about
  this file).

  This file does not hold concrete project decisions itself (e.g. "should
  payment use PostgreSQL"). Those are decision INSTANCES, filed under their
  owning domain's pending/approved/rejected/superseded state once raised
  (see Storage below). This file only defines the rules that govern that
  lifecycle — the same ENGINE-vs-MODEL pattern already used by
  tasks/tasks.sdd (STEP 8 final), chains/chains.sdd and skills/skills.sdd.


Owns:
  ./


Scope:
  Decision lifecycle
  Decision structure (contract every decision instance must satisfy)
  Approval / rejection rules
  Conflict resolution
  Decision traceability
  Decision state (instance-level — see States below)


DoesNotOwn:
  Project-specific decisions (concrete decision instances — see Storage below)
  Project-specific architecture (see project/architecture.sdd)
  Project-specific requirements (see project/)
  Tasks (see tasks/tasks.sdd)
  Skills (see skills/skills.sdd)
  Chains (see chains/chains.sdd)

  (this file records THAT a decision was needed and WHAT was decided — it
  does not own the domains a decision happens to be ABOUT, nor the concrete
  decision instances themselves; tasks/tasks.sdd -> DoesNotOwn lists
  "Project-specific tasks" for the identical reason — an ENGINE file
  excludes its own instance type; see architecture/architecture.sdd ->
  Forbidden for the SDD-system-level version of this same boundary)


Ownership:
  Every decision is identified by the component that owns it:
    project
    domain
    module
    feature (if applicable)

  [D15] Every decision MUST have a resolvable owner (domain/module) — see
        Storage below for where an owned decision instance is filed.


Storage:
  A decision instance is stored under its owning domain, NOT beside this
  file (root) — this keeps .sdd portable across projects (see Locality
  principle, architecture/architecture.sdd -> Principles). This file
  (DecisionEngine) only DEFINES this convention and the lifecycle/rules a
  decision instance must follow; it is not the storage itself — the same
  ENGINE-vs-MODEL split already applied to tasks/tasks.sdd (STEP 8 final,
  see its Storage section and Note).

  Convention:
    project/<domain>/decisions/<state>/*.sdd

  Where <state> is one of: pending / approved / rejected / superseded —
  matching the DecisionLifecycle states below.

  For example:
    project/payments/decisions/pending/DEC-004.sdd
    project/payments/decisions/approved/DEC-002.sdd

  The .sdd/decisions/ directory MUST NOT contain project decision
  instances.


DecisionTypes:

  architecture
  requirement
  security
  dependency
  technology
  scope
  process
  exception


DecisionLifecycle:

  CREATED
    >
  ANALYZED
    >
  PENDING
    >
  APPROVED

  CREATED
    >
  ANALYZED
    >
  PENDING
    >
  REJECTED

  APPROVED
    >
  SUPERSEDED


DecisionContract:

  Every decision instance MUST define:

    id
    type
    question
    reason
    context
    alternatives
    impact
    affected
    requested_by
    state
    resolution


Source:
  A decision MUST have a traceable source.

  Source MAY reference:
    prompt
    task
    architecture_change
    requirement
    bug
    another_decision

  [D16] Every decision's source MUST resolve to a real prompts/, tasks/ or
        architecture/ item (or another of the types above) — an
        abstract/undefined source is not accepted (see Source above).


Rules:

  [D1]
  AI MUST request a decision when an unresolved human choice
  affects architecture, scope, security or critical behavior.

  [D2]
  AI MUST NOT silently convert an unresolved conflict into
  an implementation decision.

  [D3]
  AI MUST provide the context required for the human to decide.

  [D4]
  AI SHOULD provide possible alternatives when known.

  [D5]
  Every approved decision MUST be traceable to the work
  that caused it.

  [D6]
  Every rejected decision MUST preserve its history.

  [D7]
  A superseded decision MUST NOT remain an active rule.

  [D8]
  A decision MAY affect project model, architecture,
  chain or task execution.

  [D9]
  Decision impact MUST be evaluated before implementation.

  [D10]
  AI MUST NOT modify unrelated project areas because of
  a decision without impact analysis.

  [D11]
  Decisions MUST remain immutable after approval.

  [D12]
  A new decision MUST supersede an old decision instead of
  silently rewriting its history.

  [D13]
  Decision lifecycle rules are defined here (.sdd/decisions/decisions.sdd),
  not inside project decision instances.

  [D14]
  Project decision instances MUST remain inside their owning project
  model (project/<domain>/decisions/<state>/*.sdd) — MUST NOT be stored
  inside .sdd/decisions/.

  [D15]
  Every decision MUST have a resolvable owner (domain/module) (see
  Ownership above).

  [D16]
  Every decision's source MUST resolve to a real prompts/, tasks/ or
  architecture/ item (see Source above).

  [D17]
  A decision MUST NOT duplicate knowledge owned by project, architecture,
  tasks or skills (see NoDuplication principle, architecture/
  architecture.sdd -> Principles).


DecisionImpact:

  local:
    affects one project component

  domain:
    affects one domain

  project:
    affects the project architecture or behavior

  global:
    affects the SDD system itself


Escalation:
  (mirrors PROJECT.sdd -> HumanDecision — this section does not redefine
  that rule, it only names the decision-layer-specific triggers that make
  [D1] fire)

  AI_CAN_DECIDE:
    routine work
    deterministic work

  AI_MUST_ASK:
    architecture conflict
    security exception
    scope change
    major technology decision
    business ambiguity
    global rule change


Resolution:

  APPROVED:
    referenced work MAY continue.

  REJECTED:
    related work MUST follow the rejection result.

  SUPERSEDED:
    the previous decision MUST NOT be used as active authority.


Traceability:

  Prompt
    >
  Analysis
    >
  Decision
    >
  Task
    >
  Chain
    >
  Implementation
    >
  Review
    >
  VR


Navigation:
  Decision lifecycle rules: @./decisions.sdd
  Project decision instances: @../project/*/decisions/
  Architecture: @../architecture/
  Project models: @../project/
  Chains: @../chains/
  Tasks: @../tasks/


States:
  (mirrors the canonical legend now defined in full at state/state.sdd,
  reinterpreted for a single decision instance's lifecycle; `^` was added
  here before state/state.sdd existed — see Note below for the update)

  ? = PENDING     (canonical "decision required" — awaiting a human choice)
  ~ = ANALYZING   (canonical "active" — context/alternatives being assembled)
  + = APPROVED    (canonical "completed" — human confirmed, dependent work may continue)
  - = REJECTED    (canonical "failed" — human declined, history preserved)
  ! = BLOCKED     (canonical "blocked" — cannot be analyzed yet, e.g. missing context)
  > = NEXT        (canonical "next" — queued to be raised with the human next)
  @ = REFERENCE   (canonical "reference" — pointer to a decision recorded elsewhere)
  ^ = SUPERSEDED  (canonical "superseded" — see state/state.sdd ->
                    ExtendedStates, where `^` is now formally defined
                    system-wide, not just added locally here; see Note)


State: +
  (STEP 9 final completed — decisions.sdd router (Rules [D1]-[D14]) is
  ENGINE only, no root-level storage directories. Concrete decision
  instances will live under project/<domain>/decisions/<state>/*.sdd once a
  real domain exists — see Note below, same restraint as STEP 5/6/7/8.)


Note:
  (prompt/new/29.md, STEP 9) Header kept as `Decisions: SDD` rather than the
  source's proposed `Spec: DecisionSystem`, for the same reason
  `tasks/tasks.sdd` kept `TaskEngine: SDD` over its own source's `Spec:
  TaskSystem`: the `<Name>: SDD` pattern (`Architecture: SDD`, `Chains:
  SDD`, `Skills: SDD`, `Prompts: SDD`) is this repo's actual naming
  convention across sibling root engine files, and "apply, don't copy"
  means the existing convention wins over a per-chunk proposal.

  `Owns`/`Scope`/`DoesNotOwn` were restructured from the source: the source
  used `Owns:` for a list of abstract concepts (decision lifecycle,
  structure, etc.), which collides with how `Owns:` is used elsewhere in
  this repo (a path, e.g. `tasks/tasks.sdd -> Owns: ./`). That concept list
  was moved to a new `Scope:` field instead, and `Owns:` now holds the path
  — the same collision-avoidance move `tasks/tasks.sdd` made when it
  renamed its own `Scope:` to `TaskScope:` to avoid colliding with the
  file's top-level `Scope:`.

  The `States:` legend was reworked from the source's proposal. The source
  reassigned `+`/`-`/`~`/`?` to decision-specific labels and introduced an
  undocumented `^` glyph while dropping `!`/`>` — but a fresh read of
  `tasks/tasks.sdd -> States` showed the actual precedent is to MIRROR the
  canonical 7-glyph legend from `PROJECT.sdd -> State` (same glyphs, same
  underlying meaning, reinterpreted for the instance type at hand) and only
  ADD a new glyph when a real gap exists, documenting the addition
  explicitly — exactly how `tasks.sdd` added `@ = reference` to its own
  States block and noted in prose that this was "not a new global marker,
  just locally completed." This file follows the same discipline: `?`/`~`/
  `+`/`-`/`!`/`>`/`@` all keep their canonical meaning, reinterpreted for a
  decision instance, and `^ = SUPERSEDED` is added on top as an explicit,
  justified extension, because none of the canonical 7 glyphs cover "was
  approved, then overridden by a newer decision."

  As with STEP 5/6/7/8, this step is deliberately skeleton + rules engine
  only. The source's `DEC-004.sdd` walkthrough (a Frontend-vs-Database
  architecture question, resolved `? -> +` after human approval of
  alternative "A: FE -> API -> BE -> DB") was NOT created as a real file
  under `pending/` — there is no concrete project domain yet to raise a
  real decision about (`project/` remains schema-only, STEP 4). It is kept
  here only as an illustration of the shape a decision instance takes once
  one exists: `Decision: DEC-004`, `Type: architecture`, `Question:`,
  `Reason:`, `Context: @project/<domain>/architecture.sdd`, `Alternatives:`
  (A/B/...), `Impact:`, `Affected: @project/<domain>`, `State: ?`,
  `RequestedBy: @task/<id>` — moving to `State: +` under `approved/` once a
  human picks an alternative.

  Most important principle (source's closing point, reformulated): this
  directory is not "AI's own decision folder." It is the layer that manages
  the points where AI needs a human decision — the mechanism that keeps
  `AI CAN DECIDE` (routine, deterministic work) separate from `AI MUST ASK`
  (architecture conflict, security exception, scope change, major
  technology decision, business ambiguity, global rule change; see
  `Escalation:` above, which applies `PROJECT.sdd -> HumanDecision` to this
  layer without redefining it).

  (prompt/new/30.md, STEP 9 correction — ROOT PURITY RULE, "Tam sıfırla",
  user confirmed) The STEP 9 (29.md) version above created four empty
  Storage subdirectories (`./pending`, `./approved`, `./rejected`,
  `./superseded`) directly under `.sdd/decisions/`. A fresh read of
  `tasks/tasks.sdd -> Storage` (STEP 8 final, 27.md) showed this repeats
  the exact mistake STEP 8 already corrected once for tasks: concrete
  instance storage does not belong at `.sdd` root, because it ties this
  otherwise-portable file to one project's data and breaks Locality/
  NoDuplication (see architecture/architecture.sdd -> Principles). The
  general rule this instance falls under: `.sdd` root MUST hold only
  system-level (engine) definitions; anything project-specific — including
  concrete decision instances — belongs under `project/<domain>/`, never
  directly under `.sdd` root (ROOT PURITY RULE).

  All four Storage directories (each holding only a `.gitkeep`) were
  deleted with user confirmation (git history preserves them). `Storage:`
  above was rewritten from a literal directory listing to an ENGINE-only
  convention statement — `project/<domain>/decisions/<state>/*.sdd` — same
  shape as `tasks/tasks.sdd -> Storage`. Two rules were added, mirroring
  `tasks.sdd`'s `[T19]`/`[T20]`: `[D13]` (lifecycle rules live here, not in
  project decision instances) and `[D14]` (project decision instances MUST
  live under their owning domain, MUST NOT be stored inside
  `.sdd/decisions/`). The `<state>` segment (pending/approved/rejected/
  superseded) is new relative to `tasks.sdd`'s convention — decisions,
  unlike tasks, have a meaningful terminal storage split by resolution
  outcome, so the state lives in the path rather than only in the
  instance's own `state:` field.

  `DecisionLifecycle`, `DecisionContract`, `Rules` `[D1]`-`[D12]`, `States`
  and `Escalation` are UNCHANGED — this correction only moves WHERE a
  decision instance is stored, not how the lifecycle works, matching how
  STEP 8 final left `TaskLifecycle`/`TaskContract`/`[T1]`-`[T17]` untouched
  when it corrected task storage.

  (prompt/new/31.md, STEP 9 enrichment — additive, Full Reset NOT required)
  The source proposed a broader rewrite of this file. Each part was checked
  against `tasks/tasks.sdd` — the sibling root-level ENGINE file this file
  was already built to structurally mirror (STEP 8 final) — and only
  adopted where that comparison actually supported it:

  - Header (`Decisions: SDD`), the `Owns:`/`Scope:` split, and the flat
    `DoesNotOwn:` list were KEPT as-is — the source's proposed
    `Spec: DecisionSystem` header, unsplit `Owns:`, and granularity-split
    `DoesNotOwn:` would all break from the convention `tasks.sdd` already
    established and this file already followed (see the 29.md Note above
    for why that convention won the first time). One genuine gap WAS
    adopted from `DoesNotOwn:`: a self-referential "Project-specific
    decisions" line was missing — `tasks.sdd -> DoesNotOwn` already lists
    "Project-specific tasks" for the identical ENGINE-excludes-its-own-
    instance-type reason, so the matching line was added here too.

  - A new `Ownership:` section was ADOPTED (renamed from the source's
    `DecisionOwnership:` to match `tasks.sdd`'s exact field name), carrying
    a new rule `[D15]` — mirrors `tasks.sdd -> Ownership`/`[T1]` exactly:
    every instance needs a resolvable domain/module owner.

  - A new `Source:` section was ADOPTED (not present in the source chunk,
    but a real gap: `tasks.sdd -> Source` requires every task's source to
    resolve to a real item, and decision instances have the identical
    traceability need — `DecisionContract` already required a `source`
    field but nothing constrained what it could point to). New rule
    `[D16]` mirrors `tasks.sdd`'s `[T2]` shape and is, like `[T2]`, both
    stated inline here and repeated in `Rules:` with a backref.

  - The source's proposed `[D15]` (a purity rule about decisions not
    redefining architecture/tasks) was REPLACED with `[D17]`, reworded to
    reference the existing `NoDuplication` principle
    (`architecture/architecture.sdd -> Principles`) directly instead of
    restating it — mirrors `tasks.sdd -> [T18]` exactly, which cites the
    same principle rather than re-deriving it.

  - `Resolution:`'s proposed new `PENDING` entry was REJECTED — `PENDING`
    is a lifecycle state (see `DecisionLifecycle`/`States`), not a
    terminal resolution outcome; adding it here would blur that
    distinction rather than clarify it.

  - `Traceability:` was FIXED (a real staleness bug, independent of the
    source's proposal): it ended `...Implementation > Verification`, while
    `tasks.sdd -> Traceability` — the pattern this chain was built to
    match — ends `...Implementation > Review > VR`. Now consistent.

  - `Navigation:` was RESTYLED from bare `@component`-name entries to the
    relative-path form `tasks.sdd -> Navigation` uses, including an
    explicit "Project decision instances" entry
    (`@../project/*/decisions/`) — the source proposed this style but its
    exact entry set was replaced with one derived from this file's own
    `Relationships`/`Traceability`/`Source` sections (Architecture, Project
    models, Chains, Tasks), following the same "what does this file
    actually reference" logic `tasks.sdd -> Navigation` uses rather than
    copying the source's list verbatim.

  - A new abstract `Portability:` section (proposed by the source) was
    REJECTED after checking `tasks.sdd -> Portability` directly: that
    section is about relocating a concrete task INSTANCE between owning
    domains (preserve history/identity, re-resolve dependencies) — it does
    not apply to decisions, which never relocate; once approved they are
    only ever SUPERSEDED ([D7], [D12]), never moved. Adding either the
    source's abstract-reusability version or a relocation-flavored
    rewrite would duplicate reasoning this file's own Storage/DoesNotOwn
    sections and `[D14]` already state.

  - The source's flat, unannotated `States:` legend replacement was
    REJECTED for the same reason the 29.md version of this section was
    already rejected once (see above) — the existing legend already
    mirrors `PROJECT.sdd -> State` correctly and stays.

  `DecisionLifecycle`, `DecisionContract`, `Rules` `[D1]`-`[D14]`,
  `DecisionImpact`, `Escalation`, `Resolution` and `States` are otherwise
  UNCHANGED — this pass only enriched structure that was either genuinely
  missing (Ownership, Source, [D15]-[D17]) or genuinely stale
  (Traceability, Navigation), following the same "apply, don't copy"
  discipline used for every prior chunk in this replay.

  (prompt/new/32.md, STEP 10) `.sdd/state/state.sdd` was written as the
  system's canonical state-notation engine. This closes a gap the STEP 9
  Note above flagged implicitly: `^ = SUPERSEDED` was added here as a
  local, additive extension "not part of the canonical 7-glyph legend"
  because no dedicated state file existed yet to hold it. It now does —
  `state/state.sdd -> ExtendedStates` formally defines `^` (alongside
  `=`/`:`/`*`/`_`) as part of the system-wide notation, not a decisions.sdd
  invention. The `States:` block above was updated to reflect this: `^`
  is now described as canonical, cross-referencing `state/state.sdd`
  instead of standing alone as a locally-justified addition. No glyph
  meaning changed — this is a provenance correction, not a redefinition.
